to his commander-in-chief, a prima donna turning into a passenger. Nelson's heart was ruling his head, but for the first time in his life, he'd become an impediment rather than an asset to the service he'd loved. Now, Tom, I totally accept that you can take a contrary view of this. Well, you can quote, suckden to a contrary view, because he also says, No one fought harder for Malta than Nelson. And that's clearly the case throughout this spell. But often he's disobeyed orders in doing so, which is... There is an example of this shortly after he has seen off the French relief force.
